Minor contact dermatitis from occupational exposure (mild irritant) to bilateral hands
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Symmetrical localized erythema and dryness, History of repeated occupational irritant exposure, Sharp demarcation corresponding to contact areas

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-013
Reason for Visit: Acute painful cracking and burning skin on both hands limiting functional use, requiring assessment for severe bacterial infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted rapidly worsening painful fissures and severe erythema on the hands, impairing activities of daily living. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate between severe cellulitis and acute irritant dermatitis, ensuring appropriate topical barrier therapy was initiated to prevent deep tissue infection before routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hands are burning, tight, and cracking open from the chemicals I use at work.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a gradual onset occurring over days to weeks of cumulative exposure, culminating in an acute flare of symptoms tonight. Symmetrical erythematous, dry, and scaly patches are confined precisely to the areas of chemical irritant contact. The patient experiences mild to moderate pruritus and a severe tight, stinging sensation in the skin. Epidermal fissuring and micro-cracking are noted in areas of high skin tension due to extreme dryness. Patient confirms repeated frequent exposure to wet work and solvents without protective gloves. There is an absence of widespread systemic rash or fever.
Functional Status: Mildly impaired fine motor tasks due to pain from skin fissuring.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational hazard exposure requires workplace modification to prevent chronicity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/76 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, frequently applying non-prescribed lotions to hands, visibly uncomfortable when clenching fists.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals symmetrical localized erythema and severe dryness on the dorsal and palmar aspects of bilateral hands. There is sharp demarcation corresponding to contact areas, directly sparing skin protected by clothing. Micro-fissuring is prominent over the metacarpophalangeal joints. There is a lack of significant weeping, crusting, or large tense bullae. Complete absence of ascending cellulitis, purulence, or fluctuance. Capillary refill is normal.
Environmental Observations: Multiple harsh cleaning agents and solvents noted in the home without protective equipment nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Using over-the-counter perfumed moisturizers with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor contact dermatitis (irritant) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Bilateral hand cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Bilateral symmetrical nature, lack of severe unilateral edema, absence of purulence, and normal temperature.

Allergic contact dermatitis - Ruled out due to: Lack of prominent weeping vesicles/bullae, and clear history of chronic mild irritant exposure rather than an acute severe allergen reaction.

Scabies - Ruled out due to: Absence of classic burrows in the web spaces and no nocturnal worsening of severe pruritus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of painful, cracking, and erythematous hands raised concern for secondary bacterial skin infection or severe acute allergic reaction. Careful bedside examination confirmed an acute exacerbation of irritant contact dermatitis directly linked to unprotected occupational exposure. Urgent assessment was necessary to explicitly rule out systemic infection, discontinue the patient's use of aggravating over-the-counter perfumed products, and initiate high-potency topical steroids to break the acute inflammatory cycle before irreversible skin thickening occurred.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of moderate-potency topical corticosteroids and a thick emollient barrier cream. Advised immediate cessation of exposure to the offending agents.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the thick emollient immediately after washing hands and patting them dry. This locks in the moisture and physically blocks further chemical irritants, directly preventing new painful cracks from forming.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nitrile or vinyl protective gloves with cotton liners.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for review of steroid response and to draft a potential occupational certificate.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the local doctor or Emergency Department if the cracks begin to ooze yellow pus, if red streaks travel up your arms, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Dermatitis is a chronic condition reliant on strict avoidance of triggers; complete resolution requires strict compliance with barrier methods.
Clinical Course: Burning and redness will diminish within 3 to 5 days of steroid use. Complete healing of deep fissures may take 2 to 3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your hands suddenly become bright red, swollen, hot, and start throbbing uncontrollably.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroid ointment directly turns off the skin's aggressive immune reaction, rapidly reducing the redness and stinging pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not use perfumed soaps. Wear cotton-lined waterproof gloves for any wet work. Apply the prescribed emollient thick like icing.

